 First, we need to find the smallest and largest three-digit integers that satisfy the congruence relation $n \equiv 7 \pmod{19}$. The smallest three-digit number is 100, and the largest is 999. We can find the smallest three-digit number that is congruent to 7 mod 19 by finding the remainder when 100 is divided by 19, and then adjusting as necessary. \[
100 \div 19 = 5 \text{ remainder } 5 \Rightarrow 100 \equiv 5 \pmod{19}
\] \[
19 \times 1 = 19 \equiv 0 \pmod{19}
\] \[
19 \times 2 = 38 \equiv 0 \pmod{19}
\] \[
19 \times 3 = 57 \equiv 0 \pmod{19}
\] \[
19 \times 4 = 76 \equiv 0 \pmod{19}
\] \[
19 \times 5 = 95 \equiv 0 \pmod{19}
\] Adding 14 to 95 (since $95 + 14 = 109$, and $109 \equiv 7 \pmod{19}$), we get the smallest three-digit number congruent to 7 mod 19: \[
95 + 14 = 109
\] Now, we find the remainder when 999 is divided by 19: \[
999 \div 19 = 52 \text{ remainder } 11 \Rightarrow 999 \equiv 11 \pmod{19}
\] To find the largest three-digit number that is congruent to 7 mod 19, we solve for $n$ in $n \equiv 7 \pmod{19}$ and $n \leq 999$: \[
n = 19q + 7, \, q \in \mathbb{Z} \Rightarrow 19q + 7 \leq 999 \Rightarrow q \leq \frac{992}{19} \approx 52.210526 \Rightarrow q \leq 52
\] The largest three-digit number that is congruent to 7 mod 19: \[
19 \times 52 + 7 = 988 + 7 = 995
\] Now, we count the multiples of 19 between 109 and 995: \[
\frac{995 - 109}{19} + 1 = \frac{886}{19} + 1 \approx 46.63 + 1 = 47
\] The number of positive three-digit integers that are congruent to $7 \pmod{19}$ is 47. \end think \] <answer> 47 </answer>

